fn rule_names(wanted: RuleEvaluation) -> String {
    IssueType::value_variants()
        .iter()
        .map(|issue_type| issue_type.text())
        .filter(|IssueText { evaluation, .. }| *evaluation == wanted)
        .map(|IssueText { name, .. }| name)
        .collect::<Vec<&str>>()
        .join(", ")
}

#[derive(Debug, Subcommand)]
pub enum HeisenbergSubcommand {
    #[command(about = "Print every rule with its identifier, section, scope and evaluation")]
    Catalogue(CatalogueCommand),
    #[command(
        about = "Report module dependency cycles and structural violations in every crate of a Cargo workspace, in the configurations it declares",
        long_about = format!("Report module dependency cycles and structural violations in every crate of a Cargo workspace, in the configurations it declares.\n\nSettings: the workspace root manifest holds every Heisenberg setting in its [workspace.metadata.heisenberg] table, whose required configurations array lists the configurations the workspace supports, each a table of a unique, nonempty name, a target among {}, default_features, which keeps or leaves out the default features, and features, the names of features that workspace members define, each listed once, no two configurations sharing their target and features, and whose optional out_dirs table declares, for a package of the workspace, member or dependency, whose build script cannot run on every host, the version of that package that the workspace resolves and a configurations table that gives, for each declared configuration, a directory, relative to the workspace root, with the files the build script writes to OUT_DIR, and the cfgs and env the build script emits; that build script then never runs, and the declared files, cfgs and env stand in for its output; its optional test_crates list names the workspace members that hold only code for tests; and its optional confined_packages table maps the name of a package, which names every resolved version of it, to the workspace members that may depend on it, directly or transitively. A missing table, an unknown key, an empty configuration list, an empty or repeated configuration name, a configuration with an unknown target, with a feature that no workspace member defines or with a feature listed twice, two configurations with the same target and features, a configuration that selects features other than the default ones in a workspace that uses resolver 1 with a root package and other members, where cargo applies a feature selection to the root package only, an out-dir for a package the workspace does not resolve, for a version of it other than the resolved ones, for a name and version that packages from several sources share or for a package without a build script, an out-dir directory outside the workspace or that cannot be read, an out-dir without the output of a declared configuration or with the output of an undeclared configuration, and a declared cfg that does not parse fail the run. A test_crates entry that is not a workspace member, that is listed twice, whose package can be published, or that a member other than a test crate depends on through a normal or build dependency is a violation, and an entry that fails these conditions is not a test crate. A confined_packages entry for a package that the workspace does not resolve, a user that is listed twice for one package or that is not a workspace member, a member other than the users of a package for which cargo builds the package in an analysed configuration, through the normal and build dependencies of any package and the member's own dev-dependencies, on the target or the host where cargo builds it, a member never reaching its own package, and a user for which cargo builds the package in no declared configuration are violations. No setting exempts code from any rule: test_crates declares which members are test code, confined_packages declares which members may depend on a package, and Heisenberg verifies both declarations.\n\nPrecondition: rustc {SUPPORTED_RUST_VERSION} compiles the workspace in every analysed configuration with the standard library of its target installed, Cargo.lock is up to date, the cargo home holds every package of Cargo.lock (`cargo fetch`), the build runs from the workspace directory with the environment Heisenberg runs with and without --config arguments, clippy runs without command-line arguments, nextest runs without the command-line arguments that replace or extend its configuration, retry tests or disable timeouts (--config-file, --tool-config-file, --retries, --debugger and --tracer), the tests run without the target selections that build and run benchmarks (--benches, --bench and --all-targets), and benchmarks run without criterion's command-line arguments that change how it measures or judges them (--quick, --sample-size, --warm-up-time, --measurement-time, --nresamples, --noise-threshold, --confidence-level, --significance-level and --profile-time). An environment variable whose name or value is not UTF-8, {rustflags_variables}, {compiler_variables}, {RUSTDOC_VARIABLE}, {CLIPPY_ARGUMENTS_VARIABLE}, {CLIPPY_CONFIGURATION_DIRECTORY_VARIABLE}, {NEXTEST_RETRIES_VARIABLE} or {nightly_feature_variables} in the environment, a workspace directory that is not the root of its cargo workspace, a workspace member or crate root outside the workspace root, a package whose metadata cargo_metadata cannot read, a crate root that two targets share or that cannot be read, a metabuild script, which has no source file, a workspace root, target directory or crate root that is not an absolute UTF-8 path, a {CLIPPY_CONFIGURATION_FILE} or {CLIPPY_HIDDEN_CONFIGURATION_FILE} that discovery finds and cannot read, or that is the only one in its directory and is not valid TOML, a {NEXTEST_CONFIGURATION_DIRECTORY}/{NEXTEST_CONFIGURATION_FILE} that cannot be read or is not a valid nextest configuration, {CARGO_RUSTFLAGS} in the [{CARGO_BUILD_TABLE}] or [{CARGO_TARGET_TABLE}] tables of the cargo configuration or in the {build_rustflags_variable} and {target_rustflags_variable} variables of a supported target, {CARGO_RUSTC}, {CARGO_RUSTC_WORKSPACE_WRAPPER}, {CARGO_RUSTC_WRAPPER} or {CARGO_RUSTDOC} in the [{CARGO_BUILD_TABLE}] table of the cargo configuration, including through the environment variables that set them, a [patch] table in the cargo configuration, whose patches belong in the workspace manifest, a build script override of a supported target in the cargo configuration ([target.<triple>.<links>]), and {CLIPPY_ARGUMENTS_VARIABLE}, {CLIPPY_CONFIGURATION_DIRECTORY_VARIABLE} or {NIGHTLY_BOOTSTRAP_VARIABLE} in the [{CARGO_ENVIRONMENT_TABLE}] table of the cargo configuration, under any letter case, fail the run. A failing run reports the first failure among loading the project, building it, resolving the standard library of its targets and reading its clippy and nextest configurations, in that order, and otherwise the analysis failures of every configuration.\n\nAnalysis: Values that the [env] table of the cargo configuration declares reach the analysis as cargo resolves them, OUT_DIR and the cfgs and env a build script emits come from running it, or from its out_dirs declaration, and an env! or option_env! of a variable that neither they nor cargo set for the target is a violation. The values of the variables that cargo sets only while it builds a target (CARGO_BIN_NAME, CARGO_BIN_EXE_<name>, CARGO_TARGET_TMPDIR and CARGO_PRIMARY_PACKAGE) are not known to the analysis, and the files that include_str! and include_bytes! name are not read. The analysis enables debug assertions, the features of the analysed configuration, each on every workspace member that defines it, as cargo does, and test only for the targets cargo builds with libtest's harness, as rustc's --test does: a target with harness = false, or one that cargo neither tests nor benchmarks, such as a build script or an example, is analysed without it. Every run analyses the declared configurations that --configuration selects, which it requires, and a --configuration the workspace does not declare or selects twice fails the run. Build scripts and procedural macros run: for each analysed configuration, cargo builds the procedural macros offline with the rustc of the sysroot and --jobs parallel jobs, which build scripts see in NUM_JOBS, and runs the build scripts, apart from those out_dirs declares, and --jobs processes of the sysroot's rust-analyzer-proc-macro-srv expand the procedural macros, whose output is analysed like any macro expansion, its input where it is written and the tokens it generates trusted, and a build script that fails or emits a cfg that does not parse, one that needs a build script out_dirs declares, a build script output that cannot be read, a procedural macro that fails to build or that the server cannot load, a declared target without its standard library, a sysroot without rustc and a sysroot without the server, or whose server cannot start, fail the run. Every decision is semantic, as rust-analyzer resolves the code. The analysis runs on --jobs worker threads and reports the same issues for every count. Shared module files are analysed in every crate that includes them. The public item, constant, method order, integration test target and benchmark target rules apply to items written in module files, including those that a macro_rules! macro of the crate writes, the item order rule to the items written in module files, where a macro call takes one place whatever it writes, and the name, tuple and parameter rules to the text written in them; an item that an attribute macro rewrites is analysed through the items of its expansion that rust-analyzer maps back to it, and the rest of the expansion, like the items that procedural macros and the macros of other crates generate, is trusted. Derive macro output is analysed like any macro expansion, except that of the built-in derives that rust-analyzer records as impls of their trait without expanding them, which is trusted; text written outside a crate's module files, such as in other crates or in the build script output that include! brings in, is trusted by the rules about how code is written, and results reflect the files on disk, including generated ones. Trust ends where the analysis would be incomplete: a name, method or macro that rust-analyzer cannot resolve and an expansion that fails are violations in all the code of a crate target, whoever wrote the macro that generates it, and unsafe code is a violation in everything the workspace wrote, wherever it expands, in what its procedural macros generate and in the build script output that include! brings in. Heisenberg does not verify what build scripts and procedural macros do when they run, the code of registry and git dependencies, the linker and the native libraries, or that the workspace compiles and passes clippy: it checks that the required lints are denied, not that the code meets them, and clippy decides by itself which code its lints skip, such as code it attributes to a macro of another crate or to a procedural macro. A partial run evaluates the rules its footer lists and exits with 0 when they find no issue. Test code is every integration test target, every target of a test crate other than a benchmark target and every module that cfg(test) gates, inline or in its own file and under any name, and benchmark code is every benchmark target, measured through criterion, the criterion package from crates.io, whose calls the criterion rules check wherever workspace code makes them; a test crate is a workspace member that test_crates lists, whose package cannot be published (publish = false), and that no member other than a test crate depends on through a normal or build dependency. The error type rules count every implementation of std::error::Error in the production code of a crate target, those that macros generate included, and accept only those that the Error derive of thiserror, the thiserror-impl package from crates.io, generates for an enum. The error type rules and the rules about where items live apply to production code and not to test code, where nobody matches on an error or finds an item by its file; `heisenberg explain` and `heisenberg catalogue` state for each rule whether it applies to all code or to production code. A standard library without the items that the analysis relies on fails the run.\n\nReport: Issues are grouped by type, in one section per type: a `<IssueType>: <count> violation(s)` line, a line that states the purpose of the rule and what to do, an empty line, and one `- <file>[:<line>:<column>]: <message>` line per issue, followed, four spaces in, by its source line when it has one and by further indented lines with its details, such as the dependencies of a cycle, the inclusions of a shared module file, the failing expansions of a macro call or the impl blocks of a flagged type. Each issue is reported once, at the name of the item it concerns; when it holds only in some of the crate targets or configurations that analyse its file, the message names them. A cause and its direct consequences form one issue. A partial run, one that leaves out a declared configuration, names the configurations it left out after the analysed ones and does not evaluate {full_run_rules}, which hold only over every declared configuration, although it does evaluate {nextest_configuration_missing_name} and {unneeded_nextest_configuration_name} when an analysed configuration runs a test under libtest's harness, evaluates only in part {partly_evaluated_rules}, whose empty-library checks hold only over every declared configuration, and names the rules it does not evaluate and those it evaluates in part; a partial run without issues exits as compliant.\n\nRules: every rule is {RULE_CATEGORY} and {RULE_DECIDABILITY}, and is listed under its section with its identifier.\n{rules}\n\nRun `heisenberg explain <ISSUE_TYPE>` for the definition of a rule, why it exists and how to satisfy it, and `heisenberg catalogue` for the scope and the evaluation of every rule.", SupportedTargetList,
            build_rustflags_variable = cargo_environment_key(&[CARGO_BUILD_TABLE, CARGO_RUSTFLAGS]),
            compiler_variables = COMPILER_VARIABLES.join(", "),
            nightly_feature_variables = NIGHTLY_FEATURE_VARIABLES.join(", "),
            rules = RuleCatalogue::new(|issue_type| !issue_type.measures_coverage()).listing(),
            full_run_rules = rule_names(RuleEvaluation::EveryDeclaredConfiguration),
            partly_evaluated_rules = rule_names(RuleEvaluation::EveryDeclaredConfigurationInPart),
            rustflags_variables = RUSTFLAGS_VARIABLES.join(", "),
            nextest_configuration_missing_name = NEXTEST_CONFIGURATION_MISSING.name,
            unneeded_nextest_configuration_name = UNNEEDED_NEXTEST_CONFIGURATION.name,
            target_rustflags_variable = cargo_environment_key(&[CARGO_TARGET_TABLE, TARGET_TRIPLE_PLACEHOLDER, CARGO_RUSTFLAGS]))
    )]
    Check(CheckCommand),
    #[command(
        about = "Report the production code of a Cargo workspace that its tests leave unexecuted, as the coverage export of a cargo llvm-cov run records it",
        long_about = format!("Report the production code of a Cargo workspace that its tests leave unexecuted, as the coverage export of a cargo llvm-cov run of one declared configuration records it.\n\nSettings: the [workspace.metadata.heisenberg] table that check reads, whose configurations and test_crates are validated as check validates them; `heisenberg check --help` describes it.\n\nPrecondition: rustc {SUPPORTED_RUST_VERSION} compiles the workspace in the measured configuration, which --configuration selects exactly once, under the preconditions of check, and --coverage-export names the JSON export that `cargo llvm-cov report --json --include-build-script` writes after a run of the tests of that configuration built from the current sources, without --remap-path-prefix, --summary-only, --skip-functions, or branch or MC/DC coverage. Every measured target is built into the instrumented run, as --lib --bins --tests --examples builds them, and executed under it: build scripts run during the build, and examples run as tests with harness = false or through cargo llvm-cov run --example, whose profiles the export merges; benchmarks never run in the test run, so the run builds no benchmark target, and an export that records regions in a module file that only benchmark targets include fails the run. An export that cannot be read, that is not JSON, not an llvm-cov JSON export in the format 3.1.0 or not made with its functions and regions, that covers another workspace, that is older than a module file of a workspace member, that names a relative file or workspace manifest, or that records branch or MC/DC coverage, a region that is not a code region, lies before the first line or column, ends before it starts or refers to a file its function does not list, a region outside the current text of its file, a region in code that the configuration leaves inactive, regions in a file of a workspace member that no crate target of the configuration includes as a module file, a module file whose metadata or modification time cannot be read, a macro call outside attributes, an attribute or a derive that rust-analyzer cannot resolve, or a macro invocation that it cannot expand or parse, anywhere in the code of a measured crate target outside test code, including the build script output that include! brings in, or production code or an attribute that only the build without cfg(test) compiles, which rust-analyzer does not analyse, fails the run, as does every failure that keeps check from loading the project.\n\nMeasurement: production code is the text written in the module files of every crate target that is neither an integration test target, a benchmark target nor a target of a test crate, libraries, binaries, build scripts and examples alike, active in the measured configuration and outside the modules that cfg(test) gates, inline or in their own file; text written outside module files, such as the build script output that include! brings in, and test code are not measured. A measured function is a function with a body whose opening brace is production code, written directly or by a macro that production code calls, a macro_rules! macro, a procedural macro that passes its input through or an attribute macro, whose expansion stands for the item it rewrites; functions written from the same text are one function, reported where their name is first written, and the functions that procedural macros generate are not measured functions, so derive macros are not expanded, nor are the macro calls inside attributes, which write no functions. The regions of the export, merged by position across every instrumented build and instantiation, each executed when any of them executed it, belong to the innermost measured function that holds the text they start at, through every macro expansion, and to each of them when several functions are written from that text, the regions of a closure to its enclosing function; a region outside every function stands alone. Every measured function holds at least one region and every region of production code is executed; there is no threshold, no exemption and no filter.\n\nReport: Issues are grouped by type, in one section per type, as check reports them, followed by the measured configuration with its crate targets, functions and regions and by the declared configurations it does not measure.\n\nRules: every rule is {RULE_CATEGORY} and {RULE_DECIDABILITY}, and is listed under its section with its identifier.\n{rules}\n\nRun `heisenberg explain <ISSUE_TYPE>` for the definition of a rule, why it exists and how to satisfy it, and `heisenberg catalogue` for the scope and the evaluation of every rule.",
            rules = RuleCatalogue::new(|issue_type| issue_type.measures_coverage()).listing()),
        mut_arg("configurations", |configurations| configurations.action(ArgAction::Set).help("Measure the coverage of this declared configuration"))
    )]
    CoverageCheck(CoverageCheckCommand),
    #[command(about = "Print the definition of a rule, why it exists and how to satisfy it")]
    Explain(ExplainCommand),
}
